[17592186536795 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536795 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186536473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536795 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e6fa46cf-d1f0-5fd4-a76c-c62db6438a9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536832 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536832 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/50/5034bb902aadd151cc1a28764e054dbdd6a694ad653f50557e5fc208c8ec2342.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above).\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50970995-9b99-52ed-ae6b-c20b930d578c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536832 #edb/ref 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nUsing $x=\\theta $ in the above identity, the integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536888 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536888 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536888 #edb/ref 1069 "q-349991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536888 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536888 #edb/ref 1071 "The total amount of money $M(t)$ in a charity’s fundraising account $t$ days after the first day of a campaign is given by the function\n\n$$\nM(t)=1500+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-27)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is measured in dollars, and $t\\ge 0$ is the time (in days) since the first day of the campaign.\n\nFind the time when the total amount is minimum, and how much money was in the account at that time.\n\nThe minimum amount is\n$$$\n{{field-1}}\nafter \n{{field-2}} days." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536888 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d80dc7c0-9327-55d2-9040-e9261d08f05c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536888 #edb/ref 1074 "To find when the total amount is a minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1500+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1500)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n3{t}^{2}-27=0\\,⇒\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how much money is in the account when $t=3,$ we figure out $M(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(3) & =1500+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1500+[{x}^{3}-27x]{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1500+[({3}^{3}-27(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =1500+[27-81] \\\\  & =1500-54 \\\\  & =1446\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1446<1500$, we conclude that the minimum amount is $$\\,1446$, and this will happen after $3$ days." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d501e7d-a406-5c5a-a298-944dab97f015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186351556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536926 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536926 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e024194-1784-50c8-99d5-8c713e4608d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536926 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186536883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536931 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e6cf617-c728-543e-ba88-85418d553d8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186332814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536935 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6658199f-07f8-5ee5-96c7-e1415acede8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186536832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536951 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b7d7adf-7ba8-5309-91c3-8926ca95fe88" #edb/ref 1759